{% doc %}
  Fork-owned icons, rendered when `snippets/icon.liquid` has no case for the name. Used inside
  an `<svg>` element, exactly like `icon.liquid`, so it is reachable through `icon-or-image`.

  These four were added for the "How it works" steps on the For Organisations page. Horizon's
  built-in set has no document-and-pencil, group-of-people or invoice glyph, and the client's
  Figma calls for all three, so they come from Tabler Icons rather than being drawn by hand.

  Attribution (MIT requires the notice to travel with the work):
    Tabler Icons v3.31.0 — https://tabler.io/icons
    MIT License, Copyright (c) 2020-2024 Pawel Kuna
    Source icons: file-pencil, users-group, file-dollar, stopwatch (+ check).

  Normalisation. Tabler draws on a 24x24 grid at stroke-width 2; `icon-or-image.liquid` opens a
  `0 0 20 20` viewBox, and Horizon's own glyphs are filled outlines one unit thick on that grid.
  So each icon is wrapped in scale(0.8333) (24 -> 20) and set to stroke-width 1.2, which lands at
  1.2 * 0.8333 = 1 unit — the same hairline as `clipboard`, `stopwatch` and the rest. `fill: none`
  on the group is required: `.icon-default` sets `fill: currentColor` on the parent `<svg>` and
  the stroke paths would otherwise fill solid. `stroke: currentColor` keeps the badge colour
  setting working.

  Because the transform assumes a 20-unit viewBox, these names belong in `icon-or-image` callers.
  Rendering them inside a 24-unit `<svg>` would draw them at five-sixths size.

  @param {string} icon - The icon name to render.
{% enddoc %}

{%- case icon -%}
  {%- when 'document_pencil' -%}
    <g
      transform="scale(0.8333333)"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1.2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
    >
      <path d="M14 3v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h4" />
      <path d="M17 21h-10a2 2 0 0 1 -2 -2v-14a2 2 0 0 1 2 -2h7l5 5v11a2 2 0 0 1 -2 2z" />
      <path d="M10 18l5 -5a1.414 1.414 0 0 0 -2 -2l-5 5v2h2z" />
    </g>
  {%- when 'people_group' -%}
    <g
      transform="scale(0.8333333)"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1.2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
    >
      <path d="M10 13a2 2 0 1 0 4 0a2 2 0 0 0 -4 0" />
      <path d="M8 21v-1a2 2 0 0 1 2 -2h4a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1" />
      <path d="M15 5a2 2 0 1 0 4 0a2 2 0 0 0 -4 0" />
      <path d="M17 10h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1" />
      <path d="M5 5a2 2 0 1 0 4 0a2 2 0 0 0 -4 0" />
      <path d="M3 13v-1a2 2 0 0 1 2 -2h2" />
    </g>
  {%- when 'invoice' -%}
    <g
      transform="scale(0.8333333)"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1.2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
    >
      <path d="M14 3v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h4" />
      <path d="M17 21h-10a2 2 0 0 1 -2 -2v-14a2 2 0 0 1 2 -2h7l5 5v11a2 2 0 0 1 -2 2z" />
      <path d="M14 11h-2.5a1.5 1.5 0 0 0 0 3h1a1.5 1.5 0 0 1 0 3h-2.5" />
      <path d="M12 17v1m0 -8v1" />
    </g>
  {%- when 'stopwatch_check' -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Tabler has no stopwatch-with-tick, so this is Tabler `stopwatch` with its minute hand
      dropped and Tabler `check` placed in the dial — both unaltered path data from the same
      set. The tick is drawn at 0.6 scale, so its stroke-width is 1.2 / 0.6 = 2 to come out at
      the same weight as the case around it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <g
      transform="scale(0.8333333)"
      fill="none"
      stroke="currentColor"
      stroke-width="1.2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
    >
      <path d="M5 13a7 7 0 1 0 14 0a7 7 0 0 0 -14 0z" />
      <path d="M17 8l1 -1" />
      <path d="M14 3h-4" />
      <path transform="translate(4.5 5.8) scale(0.6)" stroke-width="2" d="M5 12l5 5l10 -10" />
    </g>
{%- endcase -%}
